<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-geom-analysis" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Geometric Analysis</title>
 <objectives>
      <ul>
        <li><p>To understand that <term>direction fields</term> are a useful way of analyzing a differential equation from a 
        geometric point of view, especially since not all differential equations can be 
        solved analytically.</p></li>

        <li><p>To understand that an <term>autonomous equation</term> is a differential 
        equation of the form <m>y'=f(y)</m> and that phase lines can be used to 
        analyze autonomous differential equations.</p></li>
    
        <li><p>
          To understand <term>equilibrium solutions</term> to a differential equation 
          <m>y'=f(y)</m> are those solutions given by <m>f(y)=0</m> for all <m>y</m>. 
          In particular, an equilibrium solution is either a <term>sink</term>, <term>source</term>, 
          or <term>node</term>.
        </p></li>
      </ul>
  </objectives>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      As we've mentioned previously, not all differential equations -- even first-order equations -- 
      can be solved exactly. Our first goal is always to find a formula for <m>y(x)</m>. But 
      when we can't, it's nice to have a backup plan. This is where the ability to sketch 
      a graph of the solution, either by hand or with the help of a computer, can be a lifesaver.
    </p>
    <p>
      If we view the differential equation <m>y'=F(x,y)</m> as a formula for the slope of 
      a tangent line to a solution curve, we can approximate the graph of a solution curve. 
      For example, if we consider the equation <m>y'=x+y</m>, then a solution curve will have a 
      slope of 2 at the point <m>(1,1)</m>. We can use this information to obtain a 
      geometric description of the solutions to the equation.
    </p>
  </introduction>
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-More-About-Direction-Fields">
    <title>More About Direction (Slope) Fields</title>
      <p>
        Any differential equation
      </p>
      <p>
        <md>
          \frac{dy}{dx} = F(x,y)
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        can be viewed as a formula for the slope of a function <m>y=y(x)</m>. Geometrically, 
        the equation tells us that at any point <m>(x_0,y_0)</m>, the slope of a solution 
        curve is given by <m>F(x_0,y_0)</m>. Suppose that our differential equation is 
        defined on the rectangle <m>R=\{(x,y) \mid a\le x\le b, c\le y\le d\}</m> in the 
        <m>(x,y)</m>-plane. Let <m>y(x)</m> be a solution curve for <m>y'=F(x,y)</m> that 
        passes through the point <m>(x_0,y_0)</m>. Then the differential equation tells us 
        the slope of this solution curve at <m>(x_0,y_0)</m>. We can indicate this on 
        the <m>(x,y)</m>-plane by drawing a short line segment at the point <m>(x_0,y_0)</m> with 
        slope <m>F(x_0,y_0)</m>. Thus we can obtain a <term>direction field</term> or 
        <term>slope field</term> for the differential equation. A solution curve must be 
        tangent to its slope field at every point.
      </p>
      <p>
        For example, consider the differential equation <m>y'=y^2/2-x</m>. The slope 
        field for this equation is given in <xref ref="fig-slope-field-3" /> along with 
        a few solution curves.
      </p>
      <figure xml:id="fig-slope-field-3">
        <caption>The slope field for <m>y'=y^2/2-x</m></caption>
        <image source="./images/slopefield3.png" width="80%" />
      </figure>
      <p>
        Although direction fields can be tedious to compute using pencil and paper, we 
        can easily generate direction fields for any differential equation with the 
        use of computer software. Most computer algebra systems, including MATLAB, 
        have facilities for generating and graphing direction fields. For instance, 
        the following MATLAB code will generate the slope field for <m>y'=y^2/2-x</m>. 
        While we've already seen some code in the previous section that will generate 
        solution curves, we will go over the commands to do that thoroughly at the 
        end of this section.
      </p>
      <program language="octave">
        % 1. Define the differential equation as an anonymous function
        f = @(x, y) y .^2 ./2 - x;

        % 2. Set up the grid for the slope field
        x_range = 0:0.5:5; 
        y_range = -5:1:10;  
        [X, Y] = meshgrid(x_range, y_range);

        % Calculate the slopes (dx = 1, dy = f)
        dx = ones(size(X));
        dy = f(X, Y);

        % Normalize vectors so all slope lines are the same length
        L = sqrt(dx.^2 + dy.^2);
        dx_norm = dx ./ L;
        dy_norm = dy ./ L;

        % 3. Plot the slope field
        figure;
        quiver(X, Y, dx_norm, dy_norm, 0.5, 'Color', 'blue');
        hold on;

        % 4. Format the graph
        xlabel('x');
        ylabel('y');
        title('Slope Field for dy/dx = y^2/2 - x');
        axis([x_range(1) x_range(end) y_range(1) y_range(end)]);
        grid on;
        hold off;
      </program>
      <image source="./images/slopefield4.png" width="80%" />
    <p>
      There are also several sites online where you can produce sketches of slope 
      fields quickly and easily (such as the <url href="https://homepages.bluffton.edu/~nesterd/apps/slopefields.html" 
      visual="Slope Field Grapher at Bluffton University"/>.) 
    </p>
    <activity>
      <title>Plotting Slope Fields</title>
      <introduction>
        <p>
          Use MATLAB or the Bluffton University website referenced above to sketch the 
          requested slope fields
        </p>
      </introduction>
      <task>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>y'=y-2x</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>y'=y(1+x)</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>\dfrac{dy}{dx} +2xy=y</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>\dfrac{dy}{dx} = 2y(1-y/4)</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>y'=-1-y^4</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
    </activity>
  </subsection>
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-autonomous-equations">
    <title>Autonomous Equations</title>
      <p>
        We have seen that first-order differential equations can always be written in 
        the form
      </p>
      <p>
        <md>
          \frac{dy}{dx} = F(x,y) \quad \text{or}\quad \frac{dy}{dt} = F(t,y).
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>However, if the function <m>F</m> <em>only</em> depends on <m>y</m> and not on 
      <m>x</m> (or <m>t</m>), we have a special kind of differential equation.</p>
      <definition xml:id="def-autonomous-equation">
        <statement>
          <p>
            A first-order differential equation is called <term>autonomous</term> if it 
            can be written in the form
          </p>
          <p>
            <md>
              \frac{dy}{dx} = F(y) \quad \text{or} \quad \frac{dy}{dt} = F(y).
            </md>
            
          </p>
        </statement>
      </definition>
      <p>In particular, the rate of change of <m>y</m> depends <em>not</em> on <m>x</m> (or <m>t</m>),
      but only on <m>y</m>'s current value.
      </p> 
    <example>
      <p>
        The equation <m>y'=-y</m> is autonomous.
      </p>
    </example>
    <example>
      <p>
        The equation <m>y'=y^2</m> is autonomous.
      </p>
    </example>
    <example>
      <p>
        The logistic growth equation <m>P'=rP\left(1-\frac{P}{N}\right)</m> is 
        autonomous because there are no instances of the independent time variable 
        <m>t</m> on the right-hand side of the equation.
      </p>
    </example>
    <p>
      First-order autonomous equations are among the easiest to analyze; in fact, their 
      solutions have fairly limited types of behavior. Suppose that <m>y'=F(y)</m> and that 
      <m>F(r)=0</m> for some real number <m>r</m>. Then the constant function <m>y(x)\equiv r</m> is 
      a solution of the differential equation since both <m>F(y)</m> and <m>y'</m> are 
      identically zero.
    </p>
    <definition xml:id="def-equilibrium-solution">
      <statement>
        <p>
          A constant function <m>y(x)\equiv r</m>, such that <m>F(r)=0</m> is called 
          an <term>equilibrium solution</term> of the autonomous differential equation <m>\frac{dy}{dx} = F(y)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>
    <p>
      Since the slopes only depend on <m>y</m>, a slope field for an autonomous equation 
      <m>y'=F(y)</m> is completely determined once the slopes along any vertical line 
      <m>x=x_0</m> are plotted. In fact, if <m>F(y)</m> is defined and continuous for 
      all <m>y</m>, the behavior of the solutions of the equation can be determined from 
      the slope lines along the <m>y</m>-axis. This leads to the construction of what is 
      called a <term>phase line</term> for the autonomous differential equation. (See 
      <xref ref="fig-autonomous-field" />.)
    </p>
    <figure xml:id="fig-autonomous-field">
      <caption>Slope field for <m>y'=2y(1-y)</m></caption>
        <image source="./images/slopefield5.png" width="80%" />
    </figure>
    <p>
      A phase line gives us a one-dimensional picture of our solutions' increase or 
      decrease. To produce a phase line, we can use the same techniques we used back 
      in first-semester calculus to draw the intervals of increase and decrease for a 
      function <m>F</m>.
    </p>
    <note>
      <title>To draw a phase line for the equation <m>y'=F(y)</m></title>
      <p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              Find all real numbers <m>r_1\lt r_2\lt \cdots</m> such that <m>F(r_i)=0</m>, 
              and label these values on a vertical <m>y</m>-axis. These points represent the 
              equilibrium solutions. We assume first that the function <m>F</m> has finitely 
              many zeros; if not, a phase line can always be drawn using a finite interval on 
              the <m>y</m>-axis.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              For each interval <m>(-\infty, r_1), (r_1,r_2), \dots</m> pick any value <m>y=\overline{y}</m> 
              in the interval and determine whether <m>F(\overline{y})</m> is positive or negative. 
              Draw an arrow on the axis, in the given interval, pointing up if <m>F(\overline{y})</m> 
              is positive and pointing down if <m>F(\overline{y})</m> is negative. Note that if 
              <m>F</m> is a continuous function, it will have constant sign between any two zeros.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </note>
    <p>
      A solution with initial value satisfying <m>r_i\lt y(0) \lt r_{i+1}</m> is 
      monotonically increasing if the arrow points up or monotonically decreasing if 
      the arrow is pointing down. If <m>F(y)</m> satisfies the conditions of the 
      Existence and Uniqueness Theorem, then the solution must remain bounded between 
      the two equilibrium values, since they are solutions and solutions cannot 
      intersect. 
    </p>
    <example>
      <title>Drawing a Phase Line</title>
      <p>
        We will draw a phase line for the autonomous equation <m>y'=0.5(y-1)(y+2)</m>. 
        We first find the equilibrium solutions by setting the right-hand side of 
        the equation to zero. This quickly yields the two solutions <m>y\equiv 1</m> 
        and <m>y\equiv -2</m>. These are shown plotted on the phase line in 
        <xref ref="fig-phase-line-1" />.
      </p>
      <p>
        These two points split the phase line into three intervals: <m>
        (-\infty, -2), (-2, 1)</m> and <m>(1,\infty)</m>. All we need to 
        do is examine one point in each interval in order to draw the arrows 
        correctly on the phase line.
      </p>
      <p>
        For the interval <m>(-\infty,-2)</m>, we can pick, say, <m>y=-3</m> and 
        then the right-hand side of the differential equation becomes
      </p>
      <p>
        <md>
          0.5(y-1)(y+2) = 0.5(-3-1)(-3+2)=0.5(-4)(-1) = 2 \gt 0,
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        so we draw an upward pointing arrow on the phase line below <m>-2</m>. 
        We interpret this as follows: suppose have the initial condition 
        <m>y(0)=-3</m> with our differential equation. The solution curve 
        through this point must have a positive slope, but it can never cross 
        the equilibrium solution <m>y\equiv -2</m>. (Why not?) Therefore, it 
        must increase monotonically and approach <m>-2</m> as a horizontal 
        asymptote as <m>x\to\infty</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        For the interval <m>(-2,1)</m>, we can choose <m>y=0</m> and quickly 
        get that <m>F(0)=0.5(0-1)(0+2) = 0.5(-1)(2) = -1 \lt 0</m> so we draw 
        a downward pointing arrow on the interval <m>(-2,1)</m>. This means that 
        a solution curve with <m>-2\lt y(0)\lt 1</m> must be monotonically 
        decreasing and bounded between -2 and 1. Therefore it must approach 
        -2 asymptotically as <m>x\to\infty</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Lastly, for the interval <m>(1,\infty)</m>, we can choose <m>y=2</m> 
        and obtain <m>F(y) = 0.5(2-1)(2+2) = 0.5(1)(4) = 2\gt 0</m>. This means 
        that we draw an upward pointing arrow above 2 on the phase line, meaning 
        that any solution curve with initial condition <m>y(0)\gt 2</m> will 
        increase monotonically. Whether it exists for all <m>x</m> or has a 
        vertical asymptote at some positive value of <m>x</m> cannot be 
        determined geometrically.
      </p>
      <figure xml:id="fig-phase-line-1">
      <caption>Phase Line for <m>y'=0.5(y-1)(y+2)</m></caption>
      <image width="20%">
        <latex-image>
          \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.5]
            % Draw the main vertical phase line
            \draw[thick, ->] (0,-3.5) -- (0,2.5) node[above] {$y$};

            % Equilibrium points (y = 1 is stable, y = -2 is unstable)
            \filldraw[fill=black] (0,1) circle (2.5pt) node[right=4pt] {$1$};
            \filldraw[fill=black] (0,-2) circle (2.5pt) node[right=4pt] {$-2$};

            % Direction arrows based on the sign of y' = 0.5(y-1)(y+2)
            % For y &gt; 1 (e.g., y=2): y' &gt; 0 (pointing up)
            \draw[very thick, ->] (0,1.7) -- (0,1.8);
            
            % For -2 &lt; y &lt; 1 (e.g., y=0): y' &lt; 0 (pointing down)
            \draw[very thick, ->] (0,-0.4) -- (0,-0.5);
            
            % For y &lt; t' -2 (e.g., y=-3): y' &gt; 0 (pointing up)
            \draw[very thick, ->] (0,-2.7) -- (0,-2.6);
          \end{tikzpicture}
        </latex-image>        
      </image>
    </figure>
    <p>
      The phase line contains almost all of the information needed to 
      construct the graphs of solutions shown in <xref ref="fig-slope-field-6" />. It does not contain 
      information on how fast the curves approach their asymptotes, or where 
      the curves have inflection points, however. This information, which 
      does depend on <m>x</m>, is lost in going to the phase line 
      representation, but note that we did not need to solve the differential 
      equation analytically in order to draw the phase line.
    </p>
    <figure xml:id="fig-slope-field-6">
      <caption>Slope field for <m>y'=0.5(y-1)(y+2)</m></caption>
      <image source="./images/slopefield6.png" width="80%" />        
    </figure>
    </example>
    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Draw a phase line for the autonomous first-order equation <m>y'=2y(1-y)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          We set the right-hand side of the equation to 0 and solve: 
          <m>2y(1-y)=0 \Longrightarrow y\equiv 0 \text{ or } y\equiv 1</m>. 
          So we put points on our phase line at 0 and at 1. This splits our 
          line into three intervals: <m>(-\infty,0), (0, 1)</m> and <m>(1,\infty)</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          On the interval <m>(-\infty,0)</m>, we can choose <m>y=-1</m>. When 
          we substitute this into the right-hand side of our equation, we get
        </p>
        <p>
          <md>
            2y(1-y) = 2(-1)(1-(-1)) = 2(-1)(2) = -4 \lt 0.
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          and we draw a downward pointing arrow below 0.
        </p>
        <p>
          In the interval <m>(0,1)</m>, there's no getting around fractions. 
        So we choose the easiest one to work with: <m>y=\frac12</m>. This gives 
        us:
        </p>
        <p>
          <md>
            2y(1-y) = 2(\tfrac12)(1-\tfrac12) = 2(\tfrac12)(\tfrac12) = \tfrac12 \gt 0.
          </md>
          We then draw an upward pointing arrow on the phase line between 0 
          and 1.
        </p>
        <p>
          Lastly, on the interval <m>(1,\infty)</m>, we can choose an integer 
          again: <m>y=2</m>. This leads to
        </p>
        <p>
          <md>
            2y(1-y) = 2(2)(1-2) = 2(2)(-1)=-4\lt 0.
          </md>
          So we draw a downward pointing arrow above 1 on the phase line.
        </p>
        <p>
          The final product should resemble the following:
        </p>
        <image width="20%">
          <latex-image>
            \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.5]
              % Draw the main vertical phase line
              \draw[thick, ->] (0,-1.5) -- (0,2.5) node[above] {$y$};

              % Equilibrium points (y = 0 is unstable, y = 1 is stable)
              \filldraw[fill=black] (0,1) circle (2.5pt) node[right=4pt] {$1$};
              \filldraw[fill=black] (0,0) circle (2.5pt) node[right=4pt] {$0$};

              % Direction arrows based on the sign of y' = 2y(1-y)
              % For y &gt; 1 (e.g., y=2): y' &lt; 0 (pointing down)
              \draw[very thick, ->] (0,1.5) -- (0,1.4);
              
              % For 0 &lt; y &lt; 1 (e.g., y=1/2): y' &gt; 0 (pointing up)
              \draw[very thick, ->] (0,0.5) -- (0, 0.6);
              
              % For y &lt; 0 (e.g., y=-1): y' &lt; 0 (down)
              \draw[very thick, ->] (0,-0.6) -- (0,-0.7);
            \end{tikzpicture}
          </latex-image>        
        </image>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-Stability-of-Equilibrium-Solutions">
    <title>Stability of Equilibrium Solutions</title>
    <p>
      We know that equilibrium solutions of autonomous equations are <em>constant</em> 
      solutions; their graphs are horizontal lines in the plane. But what happens if 
      our initial conditions are not on the equilibrium line, but just really close?
    </p>
    <p>
      We can see in <xref ref="fig-slope-field-6" /> that if solutions start 
      initially close enough to the equilibrium solution <m>y\equiv -2</m>, they will tend toward it 
      as <m>x\to\infty</m>. On the phase line (<xref ref="fig-phase-line-1" />), 
      we can see that the arrows on either side of <m>-2</m> point toward 
      <m>-2</m>. An equilibrium solution of this type is called a <term>sink</term> and 
      is said to be a <term>stable equilibrium</term>.
    </p>
    <p>
      On the other hand, solutions starting close to <m>y=1</m> all tend to 
      move away from this solution as <m>x</m> increases. The phase line has 
      arrows both pointing away from 1. An equilibrium solution of this type 
      is said to be a <term>source</term> and is called an <term>unstable 
      equilibrium</term>.
    </p>
    <p>
      It is possible to have an equilibrium point on the phase line where an 
      arrow on one side points toward the equilibrium and an arrow on the other 
      side points away. Such an equilibrium is called a <term>node</term>. It is 
      <term>semi-stable</term> in the sense that if a solution starts on one 
      side of the equilibrium, it will tend towards it and if it starts on the 
      other side, it will move away as <m>x\to\infty</m>.
    </p>
    <example>
      <title>Drawing a phase line</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Draw a phase line for the equation
        </p>
        <p>
          <md>
            y'=y(y+1)^2 (y-3) = F(y)
          </md>
          and label each equilibrium point as a sink, source, or node.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The equilibrium solutions are the zeros of <m>F(y)</m>, namely 
          0, -1, and 3. These are plotted out in the phase line below.
        </p>
        <image width="30%">
          <latex-image>
            \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.5]
              % Draw the main vertical phase line
              \draw[thick, ->] (0,-2.5) -- (0,4.5) node[above] {$y$};

              % Equilibrium points (y = 0 is stable, y = -1 is semi-stable)
              % (y = 3 is unstable)
              \filldraw[fill=black] (0,0) circle (2.5pt) node[right=4pt] {\small $0$ (sink)};
              \filldraw[fill=black] (0,3) circle (2.5pt) node[right=4pt] {\small $3$ (source)};
              \filldraw[fill=black] (0,-1) circle (2.5pt) node[right=4pt] {\small $-1$ (node)};

              % Direction arrows based on the sign of y' = y(y+1)^2(y-3)
              % For y &lt; -1 (e.g., y=-2): y' &gt; 0 (pointing up)
              \draw[very thick, ->] (0,-1.5) -- (0,-1.4);
              
              % For -1 &lt; y &lt; 0 (e.g., y=-1/2): y' &gt; 0 (pointing up)
              \draw[very thick, ->] (0,-0.5) -- (0, -0.4);
              
              % For 0 &lt; y &lt; 3 (e.g., y=1): y' &lt; 0 (pointing down)
              \draw[very thick, ->] (0,1.6) -- (0, 1.5);
              
              % For y &gt; 3 (e.g., y=4): y' &gt; 0 (pointing up)
              \draw[very thick, ->] (0,3.75) -- (0,3.85);
            \end{tikzpicture}
          </latex-image>        
        </image>
        <p>
          We could certainly do the same analysis we did before, interval by 
          interval, to get the arrows to point the correct way. Another way 
          which we learned in first-semester calculus would be to graph 
          <m>F(y)</m> and see where it is positive or negative. (See 
          <xref ref="fig-deriv-graph" />.)
        </p>
        <figure xml:id="fig-deriv-graph">
          <caption>Graph of the slope function <m>F(y)</m></caption>
          <image source="./images/derivgraph1.png" />
        </figure>
        <p>
          It is important to realize that this graph <em>is not the graph 
          of the solution curves</em>. We only use this graph to determine 
          whether the arrow between two equilibria points up or down. We 
          can see that <m>F(y)</m> is positive between all pairs of 
          equilibrium points except 0 and 3; therefore, all of the arrows 
          point up except the one between 0 and 3. 
        </p>
        <p>
        Once the arrows are drawn, it is easy to see that -1 is a node, 
        0 is a sink, and 3 is a source. Some solutions of this equation 
        are shown in <xref ref="fig-slopefield-7" />.
        </p>
        <figure xml:id="fig-slopefield-7">
          <caption><m>y'=y(y+1)^2(y-3)</m></caption>
          <image source="./images/slopefield7.png" />
        </figure>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <activity>
      <title>Autonomous Equations and Phase Lines</title>
        <introduction>
          <p>
            For each of the differential equations below, draw the phase 
            line and classify each equilibrium solution as a sink, a source, 
            or a node.
          </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>y'=y(y-2)(y+3)</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
          <solution component="instructors">
            <p>
              The equilibrium points are found by setting <m>y'=0</m>:
              <md>
                y(y-2)(y+3) = 0
              </md>
              Therefore, the equilibrium points are <m>y = 0, 2, -3</m>.
            </p>
            <image width="30%">
              <latex-image>          
                \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.5]
              % Draw the main vertical phase line
              \draw[thick, ->] (0,-4.5) -- (0,3.5) node[above] {$y$};

              % Equilibrium points (y = -3 is source, y = 0 is sink)
              % (y = 2 is source)
              \filldraw[fill=black] (0,-3) circle (2.5pt) node[right=4pt] {\small $-3$ (source)};
              \filldraw[fill=black] (0,0) circle (2.5pt) node[right=4pt] {\small $0$ (sink)};
              \filldraw[fill=black] (0,2) circle (2.5pt) node[right=4pt] {\small $2$ (source)};

              % Direction arrows based on the sign of y' = y(y-2)(y+3)
              % For y &lt; -3 (e.g., y=-4): y' &lt; 0 (pointing down)
              \draw[very thick, ->] (0,-4) -- (0,-4.1);
              
              % For -3 &lt; y &lt; 0 (e.g., y=-1): y' &gt; 0 (pointing up)
              \draw[very thick, ->] (0,-1.5) -- (0, -1.4);
              
              % For 0 &lt; y &lt; 2 (e.g., y=1): y' &lt; 0 (pointing down)
              \draw[very thick, ->] (0,1) -- (0, 0.9);
              
              % For y &gt; 2 (e.g., y=3): y' &gt; 0 (pointing up)
              \draw[very thick, ->] (0,3) -- (0,3.1);
            \end{tikzpicture}
              </latex-image>        
            </image>
          </solution>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>y'=y^2(y-2)(y+3)</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
          <solution component="instructors">
            <p>
              The equilibrium points are found by setting <m>y'=0</m>:
              <md>
                y^2(y-2)(y+3) = 0
              </md>
              Therefore, the equilibrium points are <m>y = 0, 2, -3</m>.
            </p>
            <image width="30%">
              <latex-image>          
                \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.5]
                  % Draw the main vertical phase line
                  \draw[thick, ->] (0,-4.5) -- (0,3.5) node[above] {$y$};

                  % Equilibrium points (y = -3 is sink, y = 0 is node, y = 2 is source)
                  \filldraw[fill=black] (0,-3) circle (2.5pt) node[right=4pt] {\small $-3$ (sink)};
                  \filldraw[fill=black] (0,0) circle (2.5pt) node[right=4pt] {\small $0$ (node)};
                  \filldraw[fill=black] (0,2) circle (2.5pt) node[right=4pt] {\small $2$ (source)};

                  % Direction arrows based on the sign of y' = y^2(y-2)(y+3)
                  % For y &lt; -3 (e.g., y=-4): y' &gt; 0 (pointing up)
                  \draw[very thick, ->] (0,-4) -- (0,-3.9);
                  
                  % For -3 &lt; y &lt; 0 (e.g., y=-1): y' &lt; 0 (pointing down)
                  \draw[very thick, ->] (0,-1.5) -- (0, -1.6);
                  
                  % For 0 &lt; y &lt; 2 (e.g., y=1): y' &lt; 0 (pointing down)
                  \draw[very thick, ->] (0,1) -- (0, 0.9);
                  
                  % For y &gt; 2 (e.g., y=3): y' &gt; 0 (pointing up)
                  \draw[very thick, ->] (0,3) -- (0,3.1);
                \end{tikzpicture}
              </latex-image>        
            </image>
          </solution>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>y'=\cos y</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
          <solution component="instructors">
            <p>
              The equilibrium points are found by setting <m>y'=0</m>:
              <md>
                \cos y = 0
              </md>
              Therefore, the equilibrium points are <m>y = \frac{\pi}{2} + n\pi</m>, 
              where <m>n \in \mathbb{Z}</m>. The phase line is periodic with period <m>\pi</m> and the equilibrium points 
              alternate between sinks and sources.
            </p>
            <image width="30%">
              <latex-image>          
                \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.5]
            % Draw the main vertical phase line
            \draw[thick, ->] (0,-5.5) -- (0,6) node[above] {$y$};

            % Equilibrium points (... y = -3pi/2 stable, y = -pi/2 unstable, y = pi/2 stable, y = 3pi/2 unstable ... )
            \filldraw[fill=black] (0,-4.71) circle (2.5pt) node[right=4pt] {$-3\pi/2$ (sink)};
            \filldraw[fill=black] (0,-1.57) circle (2.5pt) node[right=4pt] {$-\pi/2$ (source)};
            \filldraw[fill=black] (0,1.57) circle (2.5pt) node[right=4pt] {$\pi/2$ (sink)};
            \filldraw[fill=black] (0,4.71) circle (2.5pt) node[right=4pt] {$3\pi/2$ (source)};

            % Direction arrows based on the sign of y' = cos y
            % For y &lt; -3\pi/2 (e.g., y=-4): y' &gt; 0 (pointing up)
            \draw[very thick, ->] (0,-5.2) -- (0,-5.1);

            % For -3\pi/2 &lt; y &lt; -\pi/2 (e.g., y=-2): y' &gt; 0 (pointing down)
            \draw[very thick, ->] (0,-3.1) -- (0,-3.2);
            
            % For -\pi/2 &lt; y &lt; \pi/2 (e.g., y=0): y' &lt; 0 (pointing up)
            \draw[very thick, ->] (0,0) -- (0,0.1);
            
            % For \pi/2 &lt; y &lt; 3\pi/2  (e.g., y=2): y' &lt; 0 (pointing down)
            \draw[very thick, ->] (0,3.2) -- (0,3.1);

            % For y &gt; 3\pi/2 (e.g., y=4): y' &gt; 0 (pointing up)
            \draw[very thick, ->] (0,5.2) -- (0,5.3);
            
           	% Draw dots to indicate pattern goes on forever
	    \node at (0.5, 5.7) {$\vdots$};
	    \node at (0.5, -5.1) {$\vdots$};

          \end{tikzpicture}
              </latex-image>        
            </image>
          </solution>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>y'=\cos^2 y</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
          <solution component="instructors">
            <p>
              The equilibrium points are found by setting <m>y'=0</m>:
              <md>
                \cos^2 y = 0
              </md>
              Therefore, the equilibrium points are <m>y = \frac{\pi}{2} + n\pi</m>, 
              where <m>n \in \mathbb{Z}</m>. The phase line is periodic with period 
              <m>\pi</m> and all equilibrium points are nodes.
            </p>
            <image width="30%">
              <latex-image>          
                \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.5]
                  % Draw the main vertical phase line
                  \draw[thick, ->] (0,-5.5) -- (0,6) node[above] {$y$};

                  % Equilibrium points (... y = -3pi/2 stable, y = -pi/2 stable, y = pi/2 stable, y = 3pi/2 stable ... )
                  \filldraw[fill=black] (0,-4.71) circle (2.5pt) node[right=4pt] {$-3\pi/2$ (node)};
                  \filldraw[fill=black] (0,-1.57) circle (2.5pt) node[right=4pt] {$-\pi/2$ (node)};
                  \filldraw[fill=black] (0,1.57) circle (2.5pt) node[right=4pt] {$\pi/2$ (node)};
                  \filldraw[fill=black] (0,4.71) circle (2.5pt) node[right=4pt] {$3\pi/2$ (node)};

                  % Direction arrows based on the sign of y' = cos^2 y
                  % For all intervals between equilibrium points, y' &gt; 0 (pointing up)
                  \draw[very thick, ->] (0,-5.2) -- (0,-5.1);
                  \draw[very thick, ->] (0,-3.1) -- (0,-3);
                  \draw[very thick, ->] (0,-0.6) -- (0,-0.5);
                  \draw[very thick, ->] (0,1) -- (0,1.1);
                  \draw[very thick, ->] (0,3.2) -- (0,3.3);
                  \draw[very thick, ->] (0,5.2) -- (0,5.3); 

                  % Draw dots to indicate pattern goes on forever
	    \node at (0.5, 5.7) {$\vdots$};
	    \node at (0.5, -5.1) {$\vdots$};
                \end{tikzpicture}
              </latex-image>        
            </image>
          </solution>

        </task>
      <conclusion>
        <p>
          In each case comment on anything that you notice about the phase 
          line and the equilibrium solutions.
        </p>
      </conclusion>
    </activity>
  </subsection>
  <exercises xml:id="exercises-geom-analysis">
    <title>Exercises</title>
    
    <exercisegroup cols="2" xml:id="plot-dir-fields-by-hand">
      <title>Plotting Direction Fields by Hand</title>
      <introduction>
        <p>
          For each of the differential equations in <xref ref="plot-dir-fields-by-hand" />, 
          plot the direction field on the integer coordinates <m>(x,y)</m> of 
          the rectangle <m>-2 \lt x \lt 2</m> and <m>-2 \lt y \lt 2</m> by 
          drawing a short line of the appropriate slope.
        </p>
      </introduction>
    
      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>y'=x+y</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <solution component="instructors">
          <p>
            The slope at each point is given by the sum of the <m>x</m>- and <m>y</m>-coordinates. 
            For example, at the point (1,1), the slope is 2; at the point (-1,1), 
            the slope is 0; and at the point (0,-2), the slope is -2. The following 
            figure shows the direction field for this differential equation.
          </p>
          <image width="80%" source="./images/exslopefield1.png" />
        </solution>
      </exercise>

      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>y'=xy</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <solution component="instructors">
          <p>
            The slope at each point is given by the product of the <m>x</m>- and <m>y</m>-coordinates. 
            For example, at the point (1,1), the slope is 1; at the point (-1,1), 
            the slope is -1; and at the point (0,-2), the slope is 0. The following 
            figure shows the direction field for this differential equation.
          </p>
          <image width="80%" source="./images/exslopefield2.png" />
        </solution>
      </exercise>

      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>y'=x^2+y^2</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <solution component="instructors">
          <p>
            The slope at each point is given by the sum of the squares of the <m>x</m>- and 
            <m>y</m>-coordinates. 
            For example, at the point (1,1), the slope is 2; at the point (-1,1), 
            the slope is 2; and at the point (0,-2), the slope is 4. The following 
            figure shows the direction field for this differential equation.
          </p>
          <image width="80%" source="./images/exslopefield3.png" />
        </solution>
      </exercise>

      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>y'=x+\tan y</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <solution component="instructors">
          <p>
            The slope at each point is given by the sum of the <m>x</m>-coordinate and the tangent of the <m>y</m>-coordinate. 
            For example, at the point (1,0), the slope is 1; at the point (-1,1), 
            the slope is <m>-1 + \tan(1)\approx 2.55741</m>; and at the point (0,-2), the slope is 
            <m>-2 + \tan(-2)\approx 0.18504</m>. The following 
            figure shows the direction field for this differential equation.
          </p>
          <image width="80%" source="./images/exslopefield4.png" />
        </solution>
      </exercise>

      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>y'=(x+y)/(x^2+y^2)</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <solution component="instructors">
          <p>
            The slope at each point is given by the ratio of the sum of the <m>x</m>- and <m>y</m>-coordinates to the sum of their squares. 
            For example, at the point (1,1), the slope is <m>2/2 = 1</m>; at the point (-1,1), 
            the slope is <m>0/2 = 0</m>; and at the point (0,-2), the slope is <m>-2/4 = -1/2</m>. The following 
            figure shows the direction field for this differential equation. (Note that we have not drawn the slope at the 
            origin, since it is undefined.)
          </p>
          <image width="80%" source="./images/exslopefield5.png" />
        </solution>
      </exercise>

      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>y'=y-x+1</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <solution component="instructors">
          <p>
            The slope at each point is given by the difference between the <m>y</m>-coordinate and the <m>x</m>-coordinate, plus 1. 
            For example, at the point (1,1), the slope is 1; at the point (-1,1), 
            the slope is 3; and at the point (0,-2), the slope is -1. The following 
            figure shows the direction field for this differential equation.
          </p>
          <image width="80%" source="./images/exslopefield6.png" />
        </solution>
      </exercise>
    
    </exercisegroup>

    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Use MATLAB to plot the slope fields for the differential equations in 
          <xref ref="plot-dir-fields-by-hand" />.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="instructors">
        <p>
          The MATLAB code to plot the slope fields for the differential equations 
          in <xref ref="plot-dir-fields-by-hand" /> is provided below. You can 
          copy and paste this code into a MATLAB script or command window to 
          generate the slope fields.
        </p>
        <program language="octave">
          <code>
            % Define the grid for x and y
            [X, Y] = meshgrid(-2:0.5:2, -2:0.5:2);

            % Define the differential equations
            F1 = X + Y; % y' = x + y
            F2 = X .* Y; % y' = xy
            F3 = X.^2 + Y.^2; % y' = x^2 + y^2
            F4 = X + tan(Y); % y' = x + tan(y)
            F5 = (X + Y) ./ (X.^2 + Y.^2); % y' = (x+y)/(x^2+y^2)
            F6 = Y - X + 1; % y' = y - x + 1

            % Normalize the slopes for better visualization
            L1 = sqrt(1 + F1.^2);
            L2 = sqrt(1 + F2.^2);
            L3 = sqrt(1 + F3.^2);
            L4 = sqrt(1 + F4.^2);
            L5 = sqrt(1 + F5.^2);
            L6 = sqrt(1 + F6.^2);

            % Plot the slope fields
            figure;
            subplot(3, 2, 1);
            quiver(X, Y, 1./L1, F1./L1);
            title('y'' = x + y');
            axis equal;

            subplot(3, 2, 2);
            quiver(X, Y, 1./L2, F2./L2);
            title('y'' = xy');
            axis equal;

            subplot(3, 2, 3);
            quiver(X, Y, 1./L3, F3./L3);
            title('y'' = x^2 + y^2');
            axis equal;

            subplot(3, 2, 4);
            quiver(X, Y, 1./L4, F4./L4);
            title('y'' = x + tan(y)');
            axis equal;

            subplot(3, 2, 5);
            quiver(X, Y, 1./L5, F5./L5);
            title('y'' = (x+y)/(x^2+y^2)');
            axis equal;

            subplot(3, 2, 6);
            quiver(X, Y, 1./L6, F6./L6);
            title('y'' = y - x + 1');
            axis equal;
          </code>
        </program>
        <image source="./images/exslopefieldsmatlab.png" width="80%" />
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercisegroup cols="2" xml:id="equil-solns-phase-lines">
      <title>Equilibrium Solutions and Phase Lines</title>
      
      
      <introduction>
        <p>
          Find the equilibrium solutions for each of the differential equations 
          in <xref ref="equil-solns-phase-lines" />. Draw the phase line for 
          each equation and classify each equilibrium solution as a sink, a 
          source, or a node.
        </p>
      </introduction>
    
      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>y'=2y-5</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <solution component="instructors">
          <p>
            The equilibrium solution is found by setting <m>y'=0</m>:
            <md>
              2y - 5 = 0 \implies y = \frac{5}{2}
            </md>
            Therefore, the equilibrium solution is <m>y = \frac{5}{2}</m>. 
            The phase line has a single point at <m>y = \frac{5}{2}</m>, and since 
            the slope is positive for <m>y &gt; \frac{5}{2}</m> and negative for 
            <m>y &lt; \frac{5}{2}</m>, this equilibrium solution is a source.
          </p>
          <image width="50%">
           <latex-image>
              \begin{tikzpicture}
                % Draw the vertical axis / phase line
                \draw (0, 1) -- (0, 4);
                \node[right] at (0, 4.2) {$y$};

                % Equilibrium points (solid dots / circles)
                \filldraw[black] (0, 2.5) circle (2.5pt) node[right, xshift=3pt] {$\frac52$ (source)};

                % Direction arrows in the intervals
                % Interval y &lt; 2.5 (e.g., around y = 2)
                \draw[->] (0, 2) -- (0, 1.6);

                % Interval y &gt; 2.5 (e.g., around y = 3)
                \draw[->] (0, 3) -- (0, 3.4);
              \end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
          </image>
        </solution>
      </exercise>

      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>\dfrac{dy}{dt} = (y-1)(y+2)</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <solution component="instructors">
          <p>
            The equilibrium solutions are found by setting <m>y'=0</m>:
            <md>
              (y-1)(y+2) = 0 \implies y = 1, -2
            </md>
            Therefore, the equilibrium solutions are <m>y = 1</m> and <m>y = -2</m>. 
            The phase line has points at <m>y = 1</m> and <m>y = -2</m>. Since the 
            slope is positive for <m>-2 &lt; y &lt; 1</m> and negative for 
            <m>y &lt; -2</m> and <m>y &gt; 1</m>, the equilibrium solution at 
            <m>y = -2</m> is a source and the equilibrium solution at 
            <m>y = 1</m> is a sink.
          </p>
          <image width="50%">
            <latex-image>
              \begin{tikzpicture}
                % Draw the vertical axis / phase line
                \draw (0, -3) -- (0, 2);
                \node[right] at (0, 2.2) {$y$};

                % Equilibrium points (solid dots / circles)
                \filldraw[black] (0, -2) circle (2.5pt) node[right, xshift=3pt] {$-2$ (source)};
                \filldraw[black] (0, 1) circle (2.5pt) node[right, xshift=3pt] {$1$ (sink)};

                % Direction arrows in the intervals
                % Interval y &lt; -2 (e.g., around y = -3)
                \draw[->] (0, -2.5) -- (0, -2.9);

                % Interval -2 &lt; y &lt; 1 (e.g., around y = 0)
                \draw[->] (0, -0.5) -- (0, -0.1);

                % Interval y &gt; 1 (e.g., around y = 2)
                \draw[->] (0, 2) -- (0, 1.6);
              \end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
          </image>
        </solution>
      </exercise>

      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>\dfrac{dy}{dx} = (y^2-1)(y-2)</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <solution component="instructors">
          <p>
            The equilibrium solutions are found by setting <m>y'=0</m>:
            <md>
              (y^2-1)(y-2) = 0 \implies (y-1)(y+1)(y-2) = 0 \implies y = -1, 1, 2
            </md>
            Therefore, the equilibrium solutions are <m>y = -1</m>, <m>y = 1</m>, and 
            <m>y = 2</m>. The phase line has points at <m>y = -1</m>, <m>y = 1</m>, 
            and <m>y = 2</m>. Since the slope is positive for <m>-1 &lt; y &lt; 1</m> 
            and negative for <m>y &lt; -1</m>, and positive for <m>1 &lt; y &lt; 2</m>, 
            and negative for <m>y &gt; 2</m>, the equilibrium solution at 
            <m>y = -1</m> is a source, the equilibrium solution at 
            <m>y = 1</m> is a sink, and the equilibrium solution at 
            <m>y = 2</m> is a source.
          </p>
          <image width="40%">
             <latex-image>
              \begin{tikzpicture}
                % Draw the vertical axis / phase line
                \draw (0, -3) -- (0, 3);
                \node[right] at (0, 3.2) {$y$};

                % Equilibrium points (solid dots / circles)
                \filldraw[black] (0, -1) circle (2.5pt) node[right, xshift=3pt] {$-1$ (source)};
                \filldraw[black] (0, 1) circle (2.5pt) node[right, xshift=3pt] {$1$ (sink)};
                \filldraw[black] (0, 2) circle (2.5pt) node[right, xshift=3pt] {$2$ (source)};

                % Direction arrows in the intervals
                % Interval y &lt; -1 (e.g., around y = -2)
                \draw[->] (0, -2) -- (0, -2.4);
                % Interval -1 &lt; y &lt; 1 (e.g., around y = 0)
                \draw[->] (0, -0.5) -- (0, -0.1);
                % Interval 1 &lt; y &lt; 2 (e.g., around y = 1.5)
                \draw[->] (0, 1.7) -- (0, 1.3);
                % Interval y &gt; 2 (e.g., around y = 2.5)
                \draw[->] (0, 2.5) -- (0, 2.9);
              \end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
          </image>
        </solution>
      </exercise>

      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>\dfrac{dy}{dx} = \sin 2y</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <solution component="instructors">
          <p>
            The equilibrium solutions are found by setting <m>y'=0</m>:
            <md>
              \sin 2y = 0 \implies 2y = n\pi \implies y = \frac{n\pi}{2}, n \in \mathbb{Z}
            </md>
            Therefore, the equilibrium solutions are <m>y = \frac{n\pi}{2}</m> for 
            all integers <m>n</m>. The phase line has points at these values, and 
            the stability alternates between sinks and sources as <m>n</m> increases.
          </p>
          <image width="30%">
            <latex-image>
              \begin{tikzpicture}
                % Draw the vertical axis / phase line
                \draw (0, -4) -- (0, 4);
                \node[right] at (0, 4.2) {$y$};

                % Equilibrium points (solid dots / circles)
                \foreach \n in {-2,-1,0,1,2} {
                  \filldraw[black] (0, {(\n*pi)/2}) circle (2.5pt) node[right, xshift=3pt] {$\frac{\n\pi}{2}$};
                }

                % Direction arrows in the intervals
                % For each interval between equilibrium points, determine the sign of sin(2y)
                % For example, between -pi/2 and 0: sin(2y) &lt; 0 (pointing down)
                % Between 0 and pi/2: sin(2y) &gt; 0 (pointing up)
                % Between pi/2 and pi: sin(2y) &lt; 0 (pointing down)
                % Continue this pattern...
                
                % Arrows for intervals
                \draw[->] (0, -3.5) -- (0, -3.9); % between -3pi/2 and -pi
                \draw[->] (0, -2.5) -- (0, -2.1); % between -pi and -pi/2
                \draw[->] (0, -0.75) -- (0, -1.15); % between -pi/2 and 0
                \draw[->] (0, 0.5) -- (0, 0.9); % between 0 and pi/2
                \draw[->] (0, 2.5) -- (0, 2.1); % between pi/2 and pi
                \draw[->] (0, 3.5) -- (0, 3.9); % between pi and 3pi/2
              \end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
          </image>
        </solution>
      </exercise>

      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>y'=(y^2+1)(y-1)</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <solution component="instructors">
          <p>
            The equilibrium solution is found by setting <m>y'=0</m>:
            <md>
              (y^2+1)(y-1) = 0 \implies y = 1
            </md>
            Therefore, the equilibrium solution is <m>y = 1</m>. The phase line has 
            a single point at <m>y = 1</m>, and since the slope is positive for 
            <m>y &gt; 1</m> and negative for <m>y &lt; 1</m>, this equilibrium solution 
            is a source.
          </p>
          <image width="50%">
            <latex-image>
              \begin{tikzpicture}
                % Draw the vertical axis / phase line
                \draw (0, 0) -- (0, 2);
                \node[right] at (0, 2.2) {$y$};

                % Equilibrium point (solid dot / circle)
                \filldraw[black] (0, 1) circle (2.5pt) node[right, xshift=3pt] {$1$ (source)};

                % Direction arrows in the intervals
                % Interval y &lt; 1 (e.g., around y = 0)
                \draw[->] (0, 0.5) -- (0, 0.1);

                % Interval y &gt; 1 (e.g., around y = 2)
                \draw[->] (0, 1.5) -- (0, 1.9);
              \end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
          </image>
        </solution>
      </exercise>

      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>y'=y^2+y+1</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <solution component="instructors">
          <p>
            The equilibrium solutions are found by setting <m>y'=0</m>:
            <md>
              y^2 + y + 1 = 0
            </md>
            The discriminant of this quadratic is <m>b^2 - 4ac = 1 - 4 = -3</m>, 
            which is negative. Therefore, there are no real equilibrium solutions 
            for this differential equation. The phase line has no points, and the 
            slope is always positive for all real values of <m>y</m>, indicating 
            that the solution curves will always be increasing.
          </p>
          <image width="30%">
            <latex-image>
              \begin{tikzpicture}
                % Draw the vertical axis / phase line
                \draw (0, -1) -- (0, 1);
                \node[right] at (0, 1.2) {$y$};

                % No equilibrium points

                % Direction arrows in the intervals
                % Since y' &gt; 0 for all y, arrows point up
                \draw[->] (0, -0.5) -- (0, -0.1);
                \draw[->] (0, 0.5) -- (0, 0.9);
              \end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
          </image>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
    
    </exercisegroup>

    <exercisegroup xml:id="sketch-solns-hand">
      <title>Sketching Solutions by Hand</title>
      
      
      <introduction>
        <p>
          Each of the differential equations in <xref ref="sketch-solns-hand" /> 
          has several initial conditions specified. Sketch the solution curves 
          that satisfy the initial conditions. Sketch your solutions for each 
          equation on the same pair of axes.
        </p>
      </introduction>
    
      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>y'=2y-5, y(0)=4, y(0)=2, y(0)=0</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>\dfrac{dy}{dt} = (y-1)(y+2), y(0)=2, y(0)=1, y(0)=0</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>\dfrac{dy}{dx} = (y^2-1)(y-2), y(0)=1, y(0)=0, y(0)=-1</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>\dfrac{dy}{dx} = \sin 2y, y(0)=2, y(0)=1, y(0)=0</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>y'=(y^2+1)(y-1), y(0)=2, y(0)=1, y(0)=0</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>y'=y^2+y+1, y(0)=1, y(0)=0, y(0)=-1</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
    
    </exercisegroup>

    <exercisegroup cols="2" xml:id="phase-line-deriv-graph">
      <title>Phase Lines from Graphs of the Derivative</title>
      
      
      <introduction>
        <p>
          Consider the differential equation <m>y'=F(y)</m>, where the graph 
          of <m>F(y)</m> is given in <xref ref="phase-line-deriv-graph" />. 
          Draw the phase line for each equation and classify each equilibrium 
          solution as a sink, a source, or a node.
        </p>
      </introduction>
    
      <exercise>
        <statement>
            <image source="./images/derivgraph2.png" width="80%" />
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <exercise>
        <statement>
            <image source="./images/derivgraph3.png" width="80%" />
        </statement>
      </exercise>
    
    </exercisegroup>
  </exercises>
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-slope-fields-MATLAB">
    <title>Plotting Slope Fields with MATLAB</title>
    <subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-Plotting-Slope-Fields">
      <title>Plotting Slope Fields</title>
      <p>
        As we've seen, a slope field for a differential equation <m>y=F(x,y)</m> 
        is comprised of lots of arrows. An arrow at a point <m>(x_0,y_0)</m> is 
        drawn with slope <m>F(x_0,y_0)</m>. Computer algebra systems such as 
        MATLAB or Octave are useful for drawing slope fields because they will 
        draw tens or hundreds of arrows without complaint.
      </p>
      <p>
        In weaponry, a group of arrows is called a <term>quiver</term>, and so 
        MATLAB uses the <c>quiver</c> command to draw an entire set of arrows 
        for a slope field. Let us plot the direction field for the differential 
        equation <m>y'=y^2/2-x</m>.
      </p>
      <program language="octave">
        % Define the differential equation function: y' = y^2/2 - x
        f = @(x, y) (y.^2)/2 - x;

        % 1. Create the grid for the slope field
        x_vec = 0:0.5:5; 
        y_vec = -5:1:10;
        [X, Y] = meshgrid(x_vec, y_vec);

        % 2. Calculate slopes and normalize vectors for uniform arrow length
        S = f(X, Y);
        L = sqrt(1 + S.^2);
        U = 1 ./ L;
        V = S ./ L;

        % 3. Plot the slope field
        figure;
        quiver(X, Y, U, V, 0.4, 'Color', [0.5 0.5 0.5]);
        hold on;

        % 4. Polish the plot layout
        xlabel('x');
        ylabel('y');
        title("Slope Field and Solutions for y' = y^2/2 - x");
        axis([0 5 -5 10]);
        grid on;
        hold off;
      </program>
      <image source="./images/slopefield8.png" width="80%" />
      <p>
      Let's discuss the MATLAB code. (We won't do this <em>too</em> often.) The first thing to know is that 
      MATLAB deals mainly with lists of numbers. So in the first line, 
      where we define the function <c>f</c>, we use the <c>.</c> before the 
      exponent to tell MATLAB to take a list <c>y</c> and square each element 
      of it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Next, we make the lists of values <c>x</c> and <c>y</c> which will 
      make up the coordinates where we will plot the arrows for the slope 
      field. We have <c>x</c> go from 0 to 5 in increments of 0.5, and <c>y</c> 
      go from -5 to 10 in increments of 1. The <c>meshgrid</c> command combines 
      the <c>x</c> and <c>y</c> lists into a matrix of coordinates <c>[X,Y]</c>. 
    </p>
    <p>
      We then form a matrix <c>S</c> of the values of the slope functions at 
      each value <c>[X,Y]</c>. Some of these numbers will be small; others will 
      be large. This would mean the arrows in the slope field would be all 
      different sizes. Therefore, we <em>normalize</em> the lengths of the arrows by 
      computing their magnitudes <c>L</c>, then dividing the components of the slope 
      by the magnitudes.
    </p>
    <p>
      At last, we draw the slope field. The <c>figure</c> command opens up 
      the graph window in MATLAB. (Always start drawing a graph with that 
      command.) We then use the <c>quiver</c> command to draw the slope field. 
      The command draws an arrow at the point <c>(X,Y)</c> of length 
      <c>U</c> in the <m>x</m>-direction and length <c>V</c> in the <m>y</m>-
      direction. We then ask MATLAB to reduce each arrow to 0.4 of its computed 
      length so that the arrows don't crash into each other. (Change that 0.4 to 
      0 to see what happens.) We then use the <c>hold on</c> so that we can add 
      on to the graph. Without it, MATLAB would erase what we have already if we 
      continue to use graphing commands.
    </p>
    <p>
      We do the final polishing of the graph; that is, we make it look nice by 
      adding in axis labels, a title, set the axes for the graph, and put in 
      a background grid.
    </p>
    </subsubsection>
    <subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-Plotting-Solutions">
      <title>Plotting Solutions</title>
      <p>
        Now let us find a numerical solution to the equation using the command 
        <c>ode45</c>. This involves 4th- and 5th-order Runge-Kutta methods and 
        returns a numerical solution (a table of values). We can add the code 
        for this into our previous program to produce the slope field along with 
        a few solutions.
      </p>
      <program language="octave">
        % Define the differential equation function: y' = y^2/2 - x
        f = @(x, y) (y.^2)/2 - x;

        % 1. Create the grid for the slope field
        x_vec = -3:0.5:5; 
        y_vec = -5:1:10;
        [X, Y] = meshgrid(x_vec, y_vec);

        % 2. Calculate slopes and normalize vectors for uniform arrow length
        S = f(X, Y);
        L = sqrt(1 + S.^2);
        U = 1 ./ L;
        V = S ./ L;

        % 3. Plot the slope field
        figure;
        quiver(X, Y, U, V, 0.4, 'Color', [0.5 0.5 0.5]);
        hold on;

        % 4. Solve and plot curves for each initial condition
        init_conds = [-1/4, 1/2, 3/2];
        x_span = [0, 5]; % Forward integration span

        % Colors for each trajectory
        colors = ['r', 'g', 'b'];

        for i = 1:length(init_conds)
            y0 = init_conds(i);

            % Forward integration from x = 0 to x = 5
            [xf, yf] = ode45(f, [0, 5], y0);

            % Backward integration from x = 0 to x = -3 (to show full curve)
            [xb, yb] = ode45(f, [0, -3], y0);

            % Combine and sort trajectories so they plot cleanly
            x_total = [flipud(xb); xf];
            y_total = [flipud(yb); yf];

            % Plot the trajectory line
            plot(x_total, y_total, colors(i), 'LineWidth', 2, ...
                'DisplayName', sprintf('y(0) = %.1f', y0));

            % Mark the initial condition point
            plot(0, y0, [colors(i) 'o'], 'MarkerFaceColor', colors(i), 'HandleVisibility', 'off');
        end

        % 5. Polish the plot layout
        xlabel('x');
        ylabel('y');
        title("Slope Field and Solutions for y' = y^2/2 - x");
        axis([-3 5 -5 10]);
        grid on;
        legend('Slope Field', 'Location', 'best');
        hold off;
      </program>
      <image source="./images/slopefield9.png" width="80%" />
      <p>
        There are other commands to solve differential equations in MATLAB (<em>e.g.</em>, <c>dsolve</c>). 
        See <url href="https://www.mathworks.com/help/symbolic/solve-a-single-differential-equation.html" 
        visual="the Solving Differential Equations page at Mathworks" />.
      </p>
      <exercises>
        <title>MATLAB Exercises</title>
        <exercise>
          <introduction>
          <p>
            Suppose that the population <m>p</m> of a trout pond can be accurately 
            modeled by the logistic equation
          </p>
          <p>
            <md>
              \frac{dp}{dt} = 0.4p\left(1-\frac{p}{500}\right).
            </md>
          </p>
          </introduction>
          <task>
            <statement>
              <p>
                Plot the direction field for this equation using MATLAB.
              </p>
            </statement>
          </task>
          <task>
            <statement>
              <p>
                Plot the graphs of two or three representative solutions 
                to this equation on the direction field.
              </p>
            </statement>
          </task>
        </exercise>
      </exercises>
    </subsubsection>
  </subsection>
  <worksheet xml:id="ws-slope-fields">
    <title>In-Class Work on Phase Lines and Slope Fields</title>
    <objectives>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            To understand how to draw a solution curve on a slope field given 
            an initial condition.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Given an autonomous first-order differential equation, be able to 
            sketch a phase line and label equilibrium solutions as a sink, source, 
            or node.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </objectives>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        If we have a slope field in hand for a differential equation, it is 
        important to be able to draw a reasonable approximation to a solution 
        curve that passes through a given point.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <page>
        <p>
            Given each direction field shown below, draw a solution curve 
            that fulfills the given initial condition (that is, a solution 
            curve that passes through the given point).
          </p>
      <exercise>
        <introduction>
          <p>
            <m>
              \dfrac{dy}{dx} = x^2-y^2
            </m>
          </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>
                y(-2)=1
              </m>
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>
                y(3)=0
              </m>
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>
                y(0)=2
              </m>
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>
                y(0)=0
              </m>
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
      </exercise>
      <image source="./images/slopefield10.png" width="80%" />
    </page>
    <page>
      <exercise>
        <introduction>
          <p>
            <m>
              \dfrac{dy}{dx} = 1-xy
            </m>
          </p>
        </introduction>
